1
00:00:01,269 --> 00:00:11,535
[SPEAKER_00]: This is a question in terms of the practice of non-resistance or peacefulness.

2
00:00:12,795 --> 00:00:22,320
[SPEAKER_00]: So, in terms of practicing peaceful, equanimous non-resistance, could this not in fact be enabling harm?

3
00:00:24,261 --> 00:00:30,705
[SPEAKER_00]: For example, if one tolerates abuse or injustice without resistance,

4
00:00:31,830 --> 00:00:35,893
[SPEAKER_00]: Does this not just allow and even encourage harmful behavior to continue unchecked?

5
00:00:37,093 --> 00:00:47,600
[SPEAKER_00]: I can understand that violence is wrong to a degree, but maybe it is justified if it is used to destroy something that causes harm to oneself and others.

6
00:00:47,620 --> 00:00:48,100
[SPEAKER_00]: So, isn't?

7
00:00:50,942 --> 00:00:52,443
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, depends what your priorities are.

8
00:00:53,964 --> 00:00:56,586
[SPEAKER_01]: If you want to live in a better world,

9
00:00:57,510 --> 00:01:02,412
[SPEAKER_01]: That's still fundamentally subjected to destruction and saṃsāra.

10
00:01:03,813 --> 00:01:20,842
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, yes, there is a line there that you can... that you can cross and defend, protect, make the society better through fighting injustice and all of that.

11
00:01:22,222 --> 00:01:23,423
[SPEAKER_01]: But that's still a worldly affair.

12
00:01:25,712 --> 00:01:33,278
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you want to practice to be free from saṃsāra, then it has to be taken in a universal sense, without exception.

13
00:01:33,859 --> 00:01:45,328
[SPEAKER_01]: So, anything worldly, including good things in the world, are an equal trap, and they have to also be abandoned.

14
00:01:46,709 --> 00:01:48,651
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why the precepts are not really negotiable.

15
00:01:51,153 --> 00:02:07,682
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, see, you're still... you will not be able to not act out of attachment, gratuitously assume possessions, craving, desire and passion, when you are fighting the injustice, when you're defending, you know, the righteous ownership.

16
00:02:08,203 --> 00:02:08,803
[SPEAKER_01]: You won't be able.

17
00:02:09,023 --> 00:02:10,404
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you have a choice.

18
00:02:10,584 --> 00:02:17,408
[SPEAKER_01]: You could be worldly righteous person, but that means you will disqualify yourself from practicing dhamma rightly.

19
00:02:18,573 --> 00:02:19,594
[SPEAKER_01]: If that's the choice you make.

20
00:02:19,774 --> 00:02:21,575
[SPEAKER_01]: No, I will defend, because it's important to me.

21
00:02:22,335 --> 00:02:25,837
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, that means abandoning the ownership that's the root of suffering and the wholesome saṃsāra.

22
00:02:25,857 --> 00:02:29,359
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not important to you, because they're mutually exclusive.

23
00:02:30,880 --> 00:02:33,422
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't have fighting and defending...

24
00:02:34,262 --> 00:02:44,288
[SPEAKER_01]: Without implicit possession, ownership, which is all kind of a mistaken already, that's mistaken sense of identity, that you are perpetuating through such acts.

25
00:02:44,348 --> 00:02:44,608
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

26
00:02:46,369 --> 00:02:47,150
[SPEAKER_00]: You are attached.

27
00:02:48,193 --> 00:02:49,074
[SPEAKER_01]: You cannot not be.

28
00:02:49,094 --> 00:02:51,716
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't start neutral, as I often say.

29
00:02:53,177 --> 00:02:54,018
[SPEAKER_01]: So, yes, exactly.

30
00:02:54,618 --> 00:02:57,181
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, freedom from saṃsāra means freedom from the world.

31
00:02:57,301 --> 00:03:02,725
[SPEAKER_01]: Just or unjust world means abandoning it all to its own means and devices.

32
00:03:02,865 --> 00:03:03,826
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not your problem.

33
00:03:05,627 --> 00:03:09,891
[SPEAKER_01]: Or, if you could choose to make it your problem, but even then you choose so because you have a desire to do so.

34
00:03:12,615 --> 00:03:13,856
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's never justifiable.

35
00:03:14,096 --> 00:03:15,856
[SPEAKER_01]: Either way, nothing.

36
00:03:15,936 --> 00:03:24,300
[SPEAKER_01]: Freedom from saṃsāra and complete arahantship is not written in the sky as a must for everyone.

37
00:03:24,380 --> 00:03:29,382
[SPEAKER_01]: Fighting for injustice, fighting against injustice in the world, it's not justifiable either.

38
00:03:30,242 --> 00:03:32,483
[SPEAKER_01]: Whatever you choose to do is because you chose to do so.

39
00:03:34,123 --> 00:03:37,745
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you want to practice for arahantship, it's on the basis of you choosing to do so.

40
00:03:39,652 --> 00:03:46,214
[SPEAKER_01]: If you want to fight for the betterment of the world, it's again because you have a desire that you follow and you make choices accordingly.

41
00:03:48,515 --> 00:03:56,077
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you recognize that it's up to you to decide, that whatever you choose, the weight of that will be on you.

42
00:03:56,817 --> 00:04:06,500
[SPEAKER_01]: So, see, if you choose to practice for saṃsāra, the freedom from saṃsāra, the weight of abandoning the world and not protecting those in need of protection and stuff, that will be on you.

43
00:04:09,042 --> 00:04:17,370
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you choose to fight for those and abandon your path of liberation against saṃsāra, well, that weight of that will be on you.

44
00:04:18,931 --> 00:04:22,835
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's up to you to decide, really, what you want and then take responsibility for it and do it.

45
00:04:29,421 --> 00:04:30,202
[SPEAKER_01]: But you can't do both.

46
00:04:31,160 --> 00:04:34,042
[SPEAKER_01]: If you're honest, you will recognize that these choices are mutually exclusive.

47
00:04:34,502 --> 00:04:35,202
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't do it.

48
00:04:35,262 --> 00:04:36,083
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't have it all.

49
00:04:37,324 --> 00:04:38,905
[SPEAKER_01]: So, which is more important to you?

50
00:04:39,645 --> 00:04:40,526
[SPEAKER_01]: A better world now?

51
00:04:42,027 --> 00:04:45,869
[SPEAKER_01]: But then, you know, the uncertain future?

52
00:04:45,889 --> 00:04:49,951
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, you know, you might well fix all the world problems and you'll die.

53
00:04:50,351 --> 00:04:50,852
[SPEAKER_01]: And what do you think?

54
00:04:50,872 --> 00:04:52,813
[SPEAKER_01]: The world will stay perfect forever after?

55
00:04:53,794 --> 00:04:57,396
[SPEAKER_01]: Greed, aversion, delusion of other individuals will not prevail, as it always does?

56
00:04:58,323 --> 00:05:09,029
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you recognize it's an immediate... immediately visible thing I can do, that can immediately verify my efforts, but it's relatively futile overall.

57
00:05:09,469 --> 00:05:16,613
[SPEAKER_01]: From a greater scheme of things, it's... it's not... it's not going to make a difference, like things, you know, change.

58
00:05:16,793 --> 00:05:17,334
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, why am I...?

59
00:05:18,594 --> 00:05:22,817
[SPEAKER_01]: Practice saṃsara, well, it's not immediately verifiable.

60
00:05:23,706 --> 00:05:32,971
[SPEAKER_01]: It might take years before you get even any insight into freedom from suffering and so on, but if you succeed, well, it's the ultimate impact.

61
00:05:35,093 --> 00:05:40,476
[SPEAKER_01]: So, there is some risk involved, but then there is a great reward.

62
00:05:41,757 --> 00:05:49,641
[SPEAKER_01]: The other one has less risk involved, because you can immediately judge your efforts of success, whether the injustice is disappearing and so on, but the reward is lesser.

63
00:05:50,121 --> 00:05:50,642
[SPEAKER_01]: It's lesser.

64
00:05:50,862 --> 00:05:52,583
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not as lasting either.

65
00:05:55,995 --> 00:06:00,816
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's really not... People pose this question as if, like, you know, well, what's the Buddhist perspective?

66
00:06:00,836 --> 00:06:03,057
[SPEAKER_01]: There's no Buddhist perspective on social issues.

67
00:06:03,877 --> 00:06:10,499
[SPEAKER_01]: As far as the Buddhist perspective goes, it's keep the five precepts for those concerned in the world that don't want to abandon the world.

68
00:06:11,359 --> 00:06:13,240
[SPEAKER_01]: The Buddha only taught suffering and freedom from it.

69
00:06:13,280 --> 00:06:14,460
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the Buddhist perspective.

70
00:06:16,861 --> 00:06:24,763
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if that's not what you're interested in, then keep the five precepts and be a good man and look after your family and, yeah, try not to harm others, but...

71
00:06:28,513 --> 00:06:30,474
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, that's the best advice.

72
00:06:30,514 --> 00:06:34,137
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the extent of the advice you can give for worldly affairs.

73
00:06:34,257 --> 00:06:34,817
[SPEAKER_01]: Doesn't matter.

74
00:06:34,857 --> 00:06:36,558
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

75
00:06:36,658 --> 00:06:39,300
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the Buddhist perspective is, free yourself from suffering.

76
00:06:41,064 --> 00:06:42,965
[SPEAKER_01]: And for that, yeah, of course, it's better.

77
00:06:43,646 --> 00:06:46,388
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you choose not to do it, then the Buddhist perspective does not apply to you.

78
00:06:47,268 --> 00:06:52,532
[SPEAKER_01]: But the reason I bring this up is because that question often comes from somebody who kind of implies both.

79
00:06:53,112 --> 00:06:55,334
[SPEAKER_01]: I am practicing ultimately, but look all of this.

80
00:06:55,514 --> 00:06:57,835
[SPEAKER_01]: Surely we can find a common ground here.

81
00:06:57,955 --> 00:07:01,398
[SPEAKER_01]: I can kill, if it's, you know, a bad person, they would defend others and stuff.

82
00:07:02,178 --> 00:07:02,659
[SPEAKER_01]: You can.

83
00:07:03,979 --> 00:07:10,444
[SPEAKER_01]: But that disqualifies you pretty severely from any successful practice of the Dhamma.

84
00:07:14,653 --> 00:07:15,994
[SPEAKER_01]: Or same like, you know, with lying.

85
00:07:16,034 --> 00:07:19,376
[SPEAKER_01]: Sometimes it's good to lie, because, you know, you might save a life and stuff.

86
00:07:19,396 --> 00:07:22,699
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, there are many factors you can start considering.

87
00:07:22,839 --> 00:07:25,121
[SPEAKER_01]: But again, is it a worldly concern?

88
00:07:28,023 --> 00:07:30,364
[SPEAKER_01]: Is it for the betterment of the world and stuff?

89
00:07:31,165 --> 00:07:34,107
[SPEAKER_01]: You save lives, but again, what will those lives be used for?

90
00:07:35,088 --> 00:07:36,809
[SPEAKER_01]: Proliferation of sensuality and stuff?

91
00:07:38,010 --> 00:07:41,973
[SPEAKER_01]: What if those lives now commit even greater harm in the future?

92
00:07:42,153 --> 00:07:43,254
[SPEAKER_01]: People make different choices.

93
00:07:44,002 --> 00:07:45,143
[SPEAKER_01]: So, now that's on you, isn't it?

94
00:07:45,603 --> 00:07:46,244
[SPEAKER_01]: The good deed.

95
00:07:47,624 --> 00:07:54,409
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's like, you're interfering, you're engaging in worldly affairs, which means there are going to be consequences, good or bad, one way or the other, you will remain bound to it.

96
00:07:54,629 --> 00:07:55,250
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the point.

97
00:07:57,751 --> 00:07:58,672
[SPEAKER_01]: If that's your choice, fine.

98
00:07:59,172 --> 00:08:01,934
[SPEAKER_01]: But don't entertain the idea that you can do both.

99
00:08:02,074 --> 00:08:11,301
[SPEAKER_00]: And not, therefore, not be bound to it, by doing worldly things, and not be bound to it, because you are Buddhist, and you imagine detachment every now and then.

100
00:08:11,321 --> 00:08:11,901
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, exactly.

101
00:08:12,001 --> 00:08:12,181
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

102
00:08:12,421 --> 00:08:13,122
[SPEAKER_01]: No, that's not...

103
00:08:15,065 --> 00:08:34,557
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I think most of the work basically just comes to developing this self-honesty, and really recognizing where that hard choice is, and then seeing, like... Because, you know, like, sometimes you have people that think they can't abandon the worldly affairs and practice rightly, because this choice was not clear to them.

104
00:08:35,197 --> 00:08:39,079
[SPEAKER_01]: Once the choice becomes clear, they realize actually they have it in them to do it.

105
00:08:41,060 --> 00:08:43,342
[SPEAKER_01]: And then you also have people that think they're doing it,

106
00:08:44,205 --> 00:08:45,786
[SPEAKER_01]: But they're doing it because the choice is not clear.

107
00:08:46,687 --> 00:08:48,849
[SPEAKER_01]: Once the choice becomes clear, they realize they can't do it.

108
00:08:49,149 --> 00:08:50,491
[SPEAKER_01]: They can't give up the world.

109
00:08:50,931 --> 00:08:56,396
[SPEAKER_00]: As long as the choice is clear, that's the... That's the important part, yeah.

110
00:08:56,456 --> 00:08:59,259
[SPEAKER_00]: Then you know that you're not... Well, then you're not fooling yourself.

111
00:08:59,299 --> 00:08:59,999
[SPEAKER_01]: You're not, yeah.

112
00:09:00,440 --> 00:09:05,364
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, again, yeah, it's the clarity, the absence of the clarity, thinking that you can do both.

113
00:09:06,145 --> 00:09:09,988
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why the person that could actually, is able to give up the world and do it.

114
00:09:10,048 --> 00:09:10,949
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why they're not doing it.

115
00:09:12,316 --> 00:09:18,939
[SPEAKER_01]: Because they're not... they haven't been told, they haven't learned, it hasn't sunk in that... no, no, I need to recognize that I can't do both.

116
00:09:19,480 --> 00:09:25,142
[SPEAKER_01]: And because of that, they're not really doing it rightly, because just... they think they can have a bit of this and a bit of that.

117
00:09:26,723 --> 00:09:34,247
[SPEAKER_01]: And the one that's thinking they're really practicing rightly, on the basis of ignoring that very clarity of their choice that needs to be made,

118
00:09:35,093 --> 00:09:38,615
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, once they recognize that, then they'll realize, oh, I don't want to do this.

119
00:09:39,276 --> 00:09:43,659
[SPEAKER_01]: If if this is required, if this choice is required for the beginning of the practice, then I don't want to do it.

120
00:09:44,199 --> 00:09:47,061
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the only reason they were doing it, is because they were not doing it.

121
00:09:48,202 --> 00:09:52,825
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's it's a it's a clear cut between worldly affairs.

122
00:09:52,845 --> 00:09:56,147
[SPEAKER_01]: You know, every every... like all those instructions in the suttas about

123
00:09:57,378 --> 00:10:14,384
[SPEAKER_01]: Given to the monks, you know, the ānāpānasati, satipaṭṭhāna, or just general jhāna instruction, those reflections, when they go into the kuti, and, you know, the kuti is empty, and then they reflect on that, and how nothing belongs to them, and nobody, and they don't belong to anybody.

124
00:10:14,404 --> 00:10:26,109
[SPEAKER_01]: And it all kind of begins with that basic mindfulness, which, as a prerequisite, the basic mindfulness, as a prerequisite, has overcoming all the joys and concerns in regard to the world.

125
00:10:26,429 --> 00:10:26,929
[SPEAKER_01]: No exception.

126
00:10:28,766 --> 00:10:30,847
[SPEAKER_01]: Completely withdrawn from it.

127
00:10:31,547 --> 00:10:35,609
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, you can start the right cultivations.

128
00:10:36,870 --> 00:10:47,755
[SPEAKER_01]: And I think, today, that whole mess between like, oh, you know, you can do both, and Buddhist perspective on this and that, and the Buddha taught lay people how to practice, same like he did the monks, which he never did.

129
00:10:49,535 --> 00:10:55,078
[SPEAKER_01]: And it's like, that all exists just because, well, this hasn't been stated hard enough.

130
00:10:56,303 --> 00:10:57,324
[SPEAKER_00]: Buddha teaches compassion.

131
00:10:57,384 --> 00:10:58,566
[SPEAKER_00]: That's whatever compassion I do.

132
00:10:58,586 --> 00:10:59,967
[SPEAKER_01]: Whatever compassion I do, yeah.

133
00:11:00,007 --> 00:11:04,452
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, I deem as the compassion that is practicing Buddhism.

134
00:11:04,653 --> 00:11:04,853
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

135
00:11:05,514 --> 00:11:08,577
[SPEAKER_01]: So, then, you could see, like, how naturally, then, you know, you can't really blame people.

136
00:11:09,038 --> 00:11:09,238
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

137
00:11:09,398 --> 00:11:13,042
[SPEAKER_01]: Because naturally, then, leads to, okay, so what's then the Buddhist perspective on this issue?

138
00:11:13,082 --> 00:11:14,144
[SPEAKER_01]: What do you do in this issue?

139
00:11:14,544 --> 00:11:15,785
[SPEAKER_01]: All completely worldly issues.

140
00:11:17,233 --> 00:11:22,580
[SPEAKER_01]: And... and usually, it kind of revolves on, like, basically, some worldly solution or the other.

141
00:11:22,600 --> 00:11:25,624
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, I remember that person once asked... well, asked somebody else.

142
00:11:27,025 --> 00:11:31,251
[SPEAKER_01]: Not me, but I was there, and he was asking, like, oh, what does the Buddhism offer?

143
00:11:31,671 --> 00:11:35,876
[SPEAKER_01]: What does the Buddhism do to all the starving children in Southeast Asia?

144
00:11:36,998 --> 00:11:43,543
[SPEAKER_01]: It was... it was maybe in the news or something, there was some big starvation, children dying, not enough food, not enough means.

145
00:11:43,603 --> 00:11:48,427
[SPEAKER_01]: What is... like, what is, you know, you being a monk, what is... what is... what does Buddhism do for that?

146
00:11:49,768 --> 00:11:56,113
[SPEAKER_01]: And this person, obviously, I don't know, maybe they got caught with that question, they just started answering, but you realize, hold on, like, that's a completely...

147
00:11:57,107 --> 00:11:58,208
[SPEAKER_01]: Completely wrong question.

148
00:11:58,728 --> 00:12:04,493
[SPEAKER_01]: It's like, would you go to a random bus driver and ask them, what does your bus driving do to starving children in Indonesia?

149
00:12:04,513 --> 00:12:05,453
[SPEAKER_01]: You wouldn't.

150
00:12:05,573 --> 00:12:08,296
[SPEAKER_01]: It wouldn't even occur to you to bring the two together.

151
00:12:08,736 --> 00:12:16,682
[SPEAKER_01]: But somehow, your life of a monk has to be doing something for that.

152
00:12:18,303 --> 00:12:23,847
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it was like, you don't ask any other profession, because it's not your problem.

153
00:12:25,220 --> 00:12:28,503
[SPEAKER_01]: But somehow it's expected that Buddhism must involve itself in this.

154
00:12:29,063 --> 00:12:30,545
[SPEAKER_01]: It must offer a perspective.

155
00:12:31,766 --> 00:12:35,229
[SPEAKER_01]: But the point is, ironically, Buddhism does a lot more.

156
00:12:37,311 --> 00:12:41,074
[SPEAKER_01]: If they were actually following the Buddhist principles, they would not have had so many children.

157
00:12:42,555 --> 00:12:44,877
[SPEAKER_01]: They would have not engaged in sexual intercourse.

158
00:12:44,917 --> 00:12:46,038
[SPEAKER_01]: They would have practiced restraint.

159
00:12:46,439 --> 00:12:47,720
[SPEAKER_01]: There would be no starving children.

160
00:12:49,161 --> 00:12:51,263
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's because people were giving in to sensuality,

161
00:12:51,855 --> 00:13:02,539
[SPEAKER_01]: Men and women alike, not thinking through, that there is not enough means for them to survive, and producing children left and right, now there is not enough food to feed, and children are dying.

162
00:13:02,699 --> 00:13:03,219
[SPEAKER_01]: It's horrible.

163
00:13:04,239 --> 00:13:08,321
[SPEAKER_01]: But it could have been prevented, if they follow the Buddhist principle, if they follow what the monk says.

164
00:13:10,642 --> 00:13:17,704
[SPEAKER_01]: Keep the precepts, and if you proliferate, if you have... if you have children, you have to do it wisely, with a purpose, with a recognition, with perspective.

165
00:13:18,784 --> 00:13:20,185
[SPEAKER_01]: Not just, well, we'll deal with it.

166
00:13:22,902 --> 00:13:26,263
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's like, it does a lot, but you have to follow it.

167
00:13:30,144 --> 00:13:35,666
[SPEAKER_01]: By this expectation, that the Buddhist teaching and the practice has to be appeasing the worldly affairs.

168
00:13:35,706 --> 00:13:36,866
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not supposed to.

169
00:13:37,546 --> 00:13:40,867
[SPEAKER_01]: Me, being a monk, it's not supposed to help starving children.

170
00:13:40,887 --> 00:13:42,588
[SPEAKER_01]: It's supposed to free me from saṃsāra.

171
00:13:44,013 --> 00:13:53,679
[SPEAKER_01]: Hopefully, enough... with enough understanding, so I can teach to others who want to do that profession, who want to choose the path of leaving the world and freeing themselves from saṃsara.

172
00:13:53,759 --> 00:13:54,160
[SPEAKER_01]: That's it.

173
00:13:54,180 --> 00:13:56,601
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a very limited application.

174
00:13:56,641 --> 00:13:57,282
[SPEAKER_01]: Taste of salt.

175
00:13:57,802 --> 00:14:00,724
[SPEAKER_01]: The sea has only one taste, which is a taste of salt, as the Buddha said.

176
00:14:01,364 --> 00:14:03,526
[SPEAKER_01]: His teaching has only one taste, which is freedom from suffering.

177
00:14:03,966 --> 00:14:05,487
[SPEAKER_01]: That's it.

178
00:14:05,527 --> 00:14:11,791
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, but how does your freedom from suffering help my architectural degree, your practice for freedom from suffering?

179
00:14:11,831 --> 00:14:13,392
[SPEAKER_01]: It's an absolutely irrational question.

180
00:14:14,675 --> 00:14:27,184
[SPEAKER_01]: Yet, it's often asked in this Buddhist circle, because the whole perversion of the values and blurring the lines, whereby now people think, no, no, no, you must, you being a monk, must also be helping this and helping that.

181
00:14:27,304 --> 00:14:34,850
[SPEAKER_00]: There is that view that a human being has responsibility to help others.

182
00:14:35,590 --> 00:14:37,852
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes.

183
00:14:38,112 --> 00:14:38,272
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

184
00:14:38,312 --> 00:14:39,513
[SPEAKER_01]: That's a worldly view.

185
00:14:39,613 --> 00:14:40,134
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

186
00:14:40,874 --> 00:14:41,454
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

187
00:14:41,495 --> 00:14:41,955
[SPEAKER_00]: But why?

188
00:14:43,915 --> 00:14:46,416
[SPEAKER_00]: There is, as I said, your duty to help others.

189
00:14:46,517 --> 00:14:54,941
[SPEAKER_01]: The only responsibility that you have is basically to recognize that you are the one responsible for your choices and desires.

190
00:14:55,001 --> 00:14:57,303
[SPEAKER_01]: There is no external factor that can justify that.

191
00:15:02,426 --> 00:15:04,647
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not saying that it's bad to help others.

192
00:15:05,467 --> 00:15:10,030
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just recognizing that it's a direction that requires

193
00:15:11,149 --> 00:15:11,609
[SPEAKER_01]: Concern.

194
00:15:12,330 --> 00:15:12,770
[SPEAKER_01]: Passion.

195
00:15:13,311 --> 00:15:13,831
[SPEAKER_01]: Attachment.

196
00:15:14,292 --> 00:15:14,812
[SPEAKER_01]: Involvement.

197
00:15:16,053 --> 00:15:20,577
[SPEAKER_00]: But that's... that... if you want to help others, what would be the best thing to do?

198
00:15:20,697 --> 00:15:21,418
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

199
00:15:21,438 --> 00:15:24,241
[SPEAKER_00]: So... Is there a path that helps others?

200
00:15:24,381 --> 00:15:25,021
[SPEAKER_00]: In a greater way.

201
00:15:25,161 --> 00:15:26,122
[SPEAKER_00]: There is no attachment.

202
00:15:26,262 --> 00:15:26,442
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

203
00:15:26,643 --> 00:15:27,183
[SPEAKER_00]: From your side.

204
00:15:27,203 --> 00:15:28,724
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

205
00:15:28,765 --> 00:15:31,267
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's... that's the kind of the whole recognition of the bigger picture.

206
00:15:31,407 --> 00:15:31,987
[SPEAKER_01]: Animal realm.

207
00:15:32,067 --> 00:15:32,648
[SPEAKER_01]: Human realm.

208
00:15:32,768 --> 00:15:37,832
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just endless... endless recurring repetition of same stuff.

209
00:15:38,686 --> 00:15:50,854
[SPEAKER_01]: A hundred years ago, five hundred years ago, same worries, same anxieties, same desires, same battle, same engagement, same injustice, same justice, same fixing, same messing up.

210
00:15:52,696 --> 00:16:03,923
[SPEAKER_01]: And, as the Buddha said, it's enough, monks, for revulsion, it's enough for dispassion, recognizing that it's more than enough to inspire you towards complete dispassion, towards the whole thing.

211
00:16:04,864 --> 00:16:06,025
[SPEAKER_01]: You just refuse to look at it.

212
00:16:06,975 --> 00:16:13,797
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the practice is really just keeping it in front of your eyes, the obvious, until dispassion will be the natural outcome.

213
00:16:14,597 --> 00:16:23,480
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the reason people are not dispassionate is they don't keep this in front of their eyes sufficiently enough, that they don't let it sink in, because it's unpleasant.

214
00:16:24,920 --> 00:16:25,980
[SPEAKER_01]: Initially, it's unpleasant.

215
00:16:27,641 --> 00:16:31,582
[SPEAKER_01]: It's like, yeah, any hope you know, any desire you have,

216
00:16:33,286 --> 00:16:35,627
[SPEAKER_01]: At least at that point in the beginning, it's bound with that world.

217
00:16:35,727 --> 00:16:40,410
[SPEAKER_01]: And now it just feels like all of that is like... None of that is worth it.

218
00:16:40,970 --> 00:16:44,132
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it is quite an impactful...

219
00:16:45,652 --> 00:16:46,893
[SPEAKER_01]: But you would come down within that.

220
00:16:47,153 --> 00:16:55,177
[SPEAKER_01]: And then you would recognize that, well, whether you are upset on account of it or not, that is factually the truth that you can observe even now.

221
00:16:56,278 --> 00:17:00,440
[SPEAKER_01]: Even in your limited experience in this life, you could already see, yeah, but how many times already?

222
00:17:02,102 --> 00:17:03,282
[SPEAKER_01]: No multiple like that, buddy.

223
00:17:05,023 --> 00:17:05,743
[SPEAKER_01]: Thousandfold.

224
00:17:05,943 --> 00:17:06,143
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

225
00:17:06,443 --> 00:17:07,164
[SPEAKER_00]: And then...

226
00:17:09,144 --> 00:17:16,047
[SPEAKER_01]: So, even if you do good and help, can you guarantee that things won't change again and won't even get worse?

227
00:17:17,007 --> 00:17:17,167
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

228
00:17:17,407 --> 00:17:18,087
[SPEAKER_01]: And you know it will.

229
00:17:19,248 --> 00:17:25,330
[SPEAKER_01]: So, one way or the other, whatever you choose to do, so you do so because you just want to fulfill your desire to do it.

230
00:17:25,950 --> 00:17:26,290
[SPEAKER_01]: That's it.

231
00:17:27,674 --> 00:17:32,416
[SPEAKER_01]: That is like kind of the conviction you have to be serving.

232
00:17:34,557 --> 00:17:41,101
[SPEAKER_01]: Whether you want it or not, that's the extent, that is the extent of your engagement, of your capacity to engage is on the basis of your desire.

233
00:17:41,961 --> 00:17:44,162
[SPEAKER_01]: Good desire, bad desire.

234
00:17:44,883 --> 00:17:46,864
[SPEAKER_01]: Still, you're pressured, you're engaged.

235
00:17:47,444 --> 00:17:49,945
[SPEAKER_01]: When that's fulfilled, you can't care even if you want to.

236
00:17:51,486 --> 00:17:52,106
[SPEAKER_01]: You will move on.

237
00:17:52,186 --> 00:17:53,207
[SPEAKER_01]: Your mind will move on.

238
00:17:55,461 --> 00:17:58,483
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if you... yeah, when the feeling changes, it's done.

239
00:17:59,824 --> 00:18:09,892
[SPEAKER_01]: So, yes, you can often, you know, some people are more compassionate than others, some people more often revolve around these things, and nobody says that's bad, but it has its expiry date.

240
00:18:12,314 --> 00:18:12,694
[SPEAKER_00]: Limited.

241
00:18:13,114 --> 00:18:14,775
[SPEAKER_01]: Limited, very limited, exactly.

242
00:18:15,596 --> 00:18:23,482
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's why the basic practice of mindfulness starts by overcoming entirety, the entirety of concerns,

243
00:18:24,438 --> 00:18:26,853
[SPEAKER_01]: There's no choice in regard to anything that comes from the world.

